// TEST: Tuple Equality Array Component (rejects)
// ERROR: BL3999
// ERROR-CONTAINS: compares as its data POINTER
// ============================================================================
// A tuple with an ARRAY component is refused in `==`, and this is the one
// refusal here that exists to prevent a WRONG ANSWER rather than a compile
// failure. `Array` promotes to its data pointer, so `std::tuple`'s member-wise
// `operator==` compares ADDRESSES: before this check the program below
// compiled, ran, and printed `same = false` for two structurally equal arrays
// -- while the same array named twice would have printed `true`.
//
// There is no honest scalar answer to give instead. Comparing arrays at TOP
// level is elementwise and yields an ARRAY of Bool (TypeCheck.boolResultTy),
// which is not something a tuple slot can hold, so the operand is refused
// rather than given a third meaning. Compare the array components directly and
// combine the results.
// ============================================================================
let x = [1.0, 2.0]
let y = [1.0, 2.0]
let t1 = x, 1
let t2 = y, 1
let same = t1 == t2
// EXPECT: typecheck failure -- an Array component has no scalar equality
